course: dictionary
lesson: 3681
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. «Flake» can be a tiny piece of something, or it can mean something coming off in small bits. The sentence around the word tells you which meaning is right, so read the whole sentence before you decide.
explain: Look at «The paint is flaking off the old gate.» Paint cannot be a snowflake here, and flaking is something that happens to paint, so flaking means coming off in small, thin pieces. Now try «Snowflakes drifted down.» Here a flake is a tiny frozen shape of ice, so the same word means something quite different.
explain: Informal words are common in speech too. «Don't flake on me!» said between friends means «Don't let me down», so do not break your promise. A dictionary shows you when a meaning is informal slang.
fact: «Flake» can be a noun (a flake, flakes) or a verb (to flake, flaking).
fact: A flake is a small, thin, flat piece of something, like a «snowflake» or «cornflakes».
fact: «Paint flakes off» means the paint comes off in small, thin pieces.
fact: Each snowflake is an ice crystal with a six-sided shape.
fact: «Flake out» can mean to fall asleep from tiredness, and «flake on» means to not do what you promised. Both are informal.
exercise: «The paint is flaking off the old gate.» What does flaking mean here? | *Coming off in small, thin pieces | Being painted a new colour | Growing taller and stronger | Shining brightly in the sun
exercise: Mum says, «Pour the cornflakes into the bowl.» What is a cornflake? | A big cold lump of ice | *A small, thin, flat piece of corn | A bird that sings at dawn | A bowl with a lid
exercise: Ben says, «Don't flake on me tomorrow!» What does flake on mean? | To paint a wall | To fall asleep after lunch | *To not do what you promised
test: «Gently flake the cooked fish with a fork.» What does flake mean in this sentence? | To wash it clean | *To break it into small pieces | To cook it for hours | To swim away
test: Dust mites eat «flakes of dead skin». What are the flakes here? | *Small, thin pieces | Long strings of wool | Shiny metal coins | Big cold lumps of ice
exam: In «Don't flake on me, Sam!», what does flake mean? | To fall asleep at once in a chair | To break into bits | *To let someone down by not keeping a promise | A tiny ice crystal
